* Why my approach works: It acts like a tally counter at a voting booth. You read each vote, add a tick mark next to the candidate's name, and then check who got more than half the votes.

3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Brute Force
Idea: Pick the first number, then scan the whole array to count how many times it appears. If it is not the majority, pick the second number and scan the whole array again.
C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        int majorityCount = nums.size() / 2;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            int count = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < nums.size(); j++) {
                if (nums[j] == nums[i]) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
            if (count > majorityCount) {
                return nums[i];
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
};

```

Time and space complexity: Time is O(n^2) because of the nested loops. Space is O(1) because no extra memory is used.
What makes the next approach better: Scanning the whole array over and over is too slow. We can just sort the numbers so identical ones sit next to each other.

Approach 2: Sorting
Idea: If a single number makes up more than half of the array, then once you sort the array from smallest to largest, that number will always cover the exact middle spot.
C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        return nums[nums.size() / 2];
    }
};

```

Time and space complexity: Time is O(n log n) because sorting takes extra time. Space is O(1) or O(log n) depending on how the sorting algorithm works behind the scenes.
What makes the next approach better: Sorting is still a bit slow. We can count them on the fly in one pass using a hash map.

Approach 3: Hash Map (Your Approach)
Idea: Create a dictionary. Every time you see a number, increase its score by 1. At the end, look for the number with a score higher than half the array size.
C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int majorityElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int, int> counts;
        int majorityCount = nums.size() / 2;
        for (int num : nums) {
            counts[num]++;
            if (counts[num] > majorityCount) {
                return num;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
};

```

Time and space complexity: Time is O(n) because we only loop through once. Space is O(n) because we create memory for every unique number.
What makes the next approach better: The hash map uses extra memory. We can solve this in O(n) time and O(1) space using a clever math trick called Boyer-Moore Voting.

Approach 4: Boyer-Moore Voting Algorithm
Idea: Imagine a game of tug-of-war. You keep track of a candidate and a score. If you see the candidate, add 1. If you see a different number, subtract 1. If the score hits 0, the next number becomes the new candidate. Because the majority element appears more than all other elements combined, it will always survive and win the game.
